// Command gen writes the review Skill from the Rules constant so the two never drift
package main

import (
	"flag"
	"fmt"
	"io"
	"os"
	"path/filepath"

	"github.com/jeon-jihyeon/nodloop/internal/diagnose"
)

const skill = `---
name: review
description: Evidence-grounded review of an event from registered data and runbooks with nodloop, with the user's verdict recorded as feedback. Use when the user asks to check, review or investigate metrics, traffic quality, anomalies or an incident, and when the user corrects a review that nodloop recorded.
---

# Review with nodloop

<!-- Generated by go generate from internal/diagnose/gen and the Rules constant in internal/diagnose/prompt.go. Edit those, not this file -->

## First setup

When no config exists the server unpacks the bundled demo data on its first start and says so on stderr. Tell the user the demo is active and that ` + "`${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/bin/nodloop setup --data-dir <dir>`" + ` points it at their own events and runbooks. Run that command yourself when the user names the directory. When the server does not start because its config is broken or has no data directory, show the user the error it printed, run ` + "`${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/bin/nodloop setup --data-dir <dir>`" + ` or ` + "`${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/bin/nodloop setup --demo`" + ` and ask the user to reconnect the nodloop server. Records go to ` + "`~/.nodloop/records`" + ` unless the config or ` + "`NODLOOP_RECORD_DIR`" + ` names another directory

## Procedure

1. Find the event. Call ` + "`events`" + ` when the user names a period or a source instead of an event id
2. Call ` + "`observe`" + ` with the event id and read the observations. Numbers come from here, never from your own arithmetic
3. Call ` + "`context`" + ` with the event id. Keep the pending id. Read the knowledge candidates and the example candidates. When ` + "`candidates_omitted`" + ` is true the lists were cut at the cap, so tell the user that more candidates exist than were offered
4. Call ` + "`select`" + ` with the pending id and the candidates that apply to this event, one reason each. Call it with empty lists when none applies. Judge relevance yourself. Do not pick the newest few by habit and do not conclude that nothing applies before reading every candidate
5. Write the review as JSON that matches the schema in the context output, from the context text and the selected text. Follow the rules below. When you summarize selected knowledge keep its units, conditions and exceptions
6. Call ` + "`record`" + ` with the pending id and the review. When it answers with ` + "`recorded`" + ` false and ` + "`revise`" + ` reasons, fix only what the reasons name and call ` + "`record`" + ` again with the same pending id. Show the user the review that record returns, never the draft. record may turn the review into a hold
7. When the user corrects or approves the review, call ` + "`feedback`" + ` with the trace id that record returned, never the pending id, the verdict approve, edit or reject, the reason in the user's words and the corrected review in full when the verdict is edit
8. After a correction that would help a later similar event, call ` + "`propose`" + ` with the reusable part: a meaning of the data or a judgment rule, its scope as change contexts, metrics and dims such as platform ios, and the trace id that record returned as evidence. Tell the user it is a candidate until they approve it. Call ` + "`approve`" + ` only when the user says so and gives their name
9. When the user reports what a real check found, call ` + "`outcome`" + ` with the trace id that record returned and confirmed, refuted or inconclusive
10. Call ` + "`detail`" + ` only when the user asks for the rows behind an observation

## Rules

%s

## Output

- Show status, observations, causes with their paragraph ids, checks in order with their purpose, open questions and hold reasons
- Say that the review is recorded and give the trace id
- Never show a review that was not returned by record
`

func main() {
	os.Exit(run(os.Args[1:], os.Stderr))
}

// Returns the exit code so only main exits the process
func run(args []string, stderr io.Writer) int {
	flags := flag.NewFlagSet("gen", flag.ContinueOnError)
	flags.SetOutput(stderr)
	out := flags.String("out", "SKILL.md", "output path")
	if err := flags.Parse(args); err != nil {
		return 2
	}
	if err := write(*out); err != nil {
		fmt.Fprintln(stderr, "gen:", err)
		return 1
	}
	return 0
}

func write(out string) error {
	if err := os.MkdirAll(filepath.Dir(out), 0o755); err != nil {
		return err
	}
	return os.WriteFile(out, []byte(fmt.Sprintf(skill, diagnose.Rules)), 0o644)
}
